# orch stale-lock takeover, after upstream #733 (fixes #730), with the leftover-marker defect
# that PR introduces removed.
#
# Replacing a dead holder's lock was unlink-then-create, so two takers that read the same dead
# pid could both end up holding the store. #733 serialises the takeover through a `wx` marker,
# .orch.lock.takeover, but a taker that dies holding it leaves it behind and every later
# takeover then reports the dead pid forever. Here:
#   - a marker older than TAKEOVER_MARKER_STALE_MS (10 s) is stepped past, not trusted. It is
#     never unlinked to make room, since two takers that both judged it stale could each unlink
#     it and the second would remove the first one's fresh marker; the next marker is named
#     after the stale one's inode and mtime so exactly one taker can create it, and the stale
#     markers are removed on release only while they are still the files judged stale
#   - holding the marker, the taker re-reads the lock and replaces it only if it still names the
#     holder it judged dead (or forced), so a taker that finished first keeps its lock
#   - heldBy is awaited inside the try whose finally awaits, so its rejection is never unhandled
#
